        "sections": [
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>To divide square roots, we simply divide the numbers under the root. This is called the <strong>quotient rule for radicals</strong>.</p>",
            "readable_text": "To divide square roots, we simply divide the numbers under the root. This is called the quotient rule for radicals.",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>To demonstrate, let&#x27;s simplify the expression <span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\dfrac{ \\sqrt{10} }{ \\sqrt{2} }]</span> by applying the quotient rule:</p>",
            "readable_text": "To demonstrate, let's simplify the expression [MATH: \\dfrac{ \\sqrt{10} }{ \\sqrt{2} }] by applying the quotient rule:",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\dfrac{ \\sqrt{10} }{ \\sqrt{2} } = \\sqrt{ \\dfrac{10}{2} } = \\sqrt{5}]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "[MATH: \\dfrac{ \\sqrt{10} }{ \\sqrt{2} } = \\sqrt{ \\dfrac{10}{2} } = \\sqrt{5}]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>The quotient rule also works for cube roots. For example,</p>",
            "readable_text": "The quotient rule also works for cube roots. For example,",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\dfrac{ \\sqrt[3]{10} }{ \\sqrt[3]{2} } = \\sqrt[3]{ \\dfrac{10}{2} } = \\sqrt[3]{5}]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "[MATH: \\dfrac{ \\sqrt[3]{10} }{ \\sqrt[3]{2} } = \\sqrt[3]{ \\dfrac{10}{2} } = \\sqrt[3]{5}]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>In general, the quotient rule works for any quotient of <span class=\"math\">[MATH: n]</span>th roots. So for any <span class=\"math\">[MATH: n,]</span> we have</p>",
            "readable_text": "In general, the quotient rule works for any quotient of [MATH: n]th roots. So for any [MATH: n,] we have",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\dfrac{ \\sqrt[n]{10} }{ \\sqrt[n]{2} } = \\sqrt[n]{ \\dfrac{10}{2} } = \\sqrt[n]{5}.]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "[MATH: \\dfrac{ \\sqrt[n]{10} }{ \\sqrt[n]{2} } = \\sqrt[n]{ \\dfrac{10}{2} } = \\sqrt[n]{5}.]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p><strong>Watch out!</strong> We cannot use the quotient rule when we have the quotient of different radicals, such as square roots and cube roots. For example, the expression <span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\dfrac{ \\sqrt{10} }{ \\sqrt[3]{5} }]</span> cannot be simplified because the radicals <span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\sqrt{\\phantom{x}}]</span> and <span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\sqrt[3]{\\phantom{x}}]</span> are different.</p>",
            "readable_text": "Watch out! We cannot use the quotient rule when we have the quotient of different radicals, such as square roots and cube roots. For example, the expression [MATH: \\dfrac{ \\sqrt{10} }{ \\sqrt[3]{5} }] cannot be simplified because the radicals [MATH: \\sqrt{\\phantom{x}}] and [MATH: \\sqrt[3]{\\phantom{x}}] are different.",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          }
        ],
        "images": [],
        "free_entry_blanks": [],
        "select_lists": [],
        "readable_text": "Content:\nTo divide square roots, we simply divide the numbers under the root. This is called the quotient rule for radicals.\n\nContent:\nTo demonstrate, let's simplify the expression [MATH: \\dfrac{ \\sqrt{10} }{ \\sqrt{2} }] by applying the quotient rule:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: \\dfrac{ \\sqrt{10} }{ \\sqrt{2} } = \\sqrt{ \\dfrac{10}{2} } = \\sqrt{5}]\n\nContent:\nThe quotient rule also works for cube roots. For example,\n\nContent:\n[MATH: \\dfrac{ \\sqrt[3]{10} }{ \\sqrt[3]{2} } = \\sqrt[3]{ \\dfrac{10}{2} } = \\sqrt[3]{5}]\n\nContent:\nIn general, the quotient rule works for any quotient of [MATH: n]th roots. So for any [MATH: n,] we have\n\nContent:\n[MATH: \\dfrac{ \\sqrt[n]{10} }{ \\sqrt[n]{2} } = \\sqrt[n]{ \\dfrac{10}{2} } = \\sqrt[n]{5}.]\n\nContent:\nWatch out! We cannot use the quotient rule when we have the quotient of different radicals, such as square roots and cube roots. For example, the expression [MATH: \\dfrac{ \\sqrt{10} }{ \\sqrt[3]{5} }] cannot be simplified because the radicals [MATH: \\sqrt{\\phantom{x}}] and [MATH: \\sqrt[3]{\\phantom{x}}] are different."
      },
