    "readable_text": "Item 1 \u2014 Tutorial: Introduction\n\nContent:\nTo divide square roots, we simply divide the numbers under the root. This is called the quotient rule for radicals.\n\nContent:\nTo demonstrate, let's simplify the expression [MATH: \\dfrac{ \\sqrt{10} }{ \\sqrt{2} }] by applying the quotient rule:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: \\dfrac{ \\sqrt{10} }{ \\sqrt{2} } = \\sqrt{ \\dfrac{10}{2} } = \\sqrt{5}]\n\nContent:\nThe quotient rule also works for cube roots. For example,\n\nContent:\n[MATH: \\dfrac{ \\sqrt[3]{10} }{ \\sqrt[3]{2} } = \\sqrt[3]{ \\dfrac{10}{2} } = \\sqrt[3]{5}]\n\nContent:\nIn general, the quotient rule works for any quotient of [MATH: n]th roots. So for any [MATH: n,] we have\n\nContent:\n[MATH: \\dfrac{ \\sqrt[n]{10} }{ \\sqrt[n]{2} } = \\sqrt[n]{ \\dfrac{10}{2} } = \\sqrt[n]{5}.]\n\nContent:\nWatch out! We cannot use the quotient rule when we have the quotient of different radicals, such as square roots and cube roots. For example, the expression [MATH: \\dfrac{ \\sqrt{10} }{ \\sqrt[3]{5} }] cannot be simplified because the radicals [MATH: \\sqrt{\\phantom{x}}] and [MATH: \\sqrt[3]{\\phantom{x}}] are different.\n\n---\n\nItem 2 \u2014 Example: Separating a Square Root Into a Quotient of Two Square Roots\n\nExample Prompt:\nExpress [MATH: \\sqrt{\\dfrac{2}{7}}] as the quotient of two radicals.\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nApplying the quotient rule, we have\n[MATH: \\sqrt{\\dfrac{2}{7}}=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{7}} .]\n\n---\n\nItem 3 \u2014 Question 1\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: sqrt((7)/(4)) =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (sqrt(7))/(sqrt(2))]\nb. [MATH: (7)/(2)]\nc. [MATH: (sqrt(7))/(2)]\nd. [MATH: (7)/(sqrt(2))]\ne. [MATH: (sqrt(7))/(4)]\n\n---\n\nItem 4 \u2014 Question 2\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: sqrt((3)/(5)) =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (sqrt(3))/(sqrt(5))]\nb. [MATH: (9)/(sqrt(5))]\nc. [MATH: (sqrt(3))/(15)]\nd. [MATH: (sqrt(3))/(5)]\ne. [MATH: (3)/(sqrt(5))]\n\n---\n\nItem 5 \u2014 Example: Writing a Quotient of Two Square Roots as a Single Square Root\n\nExample Prompt:\nExpress [MATH: \\dfrac{\\sqrt{12}}{\\sqrt{3}}] as a single radical.\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nUsing the quotient rule, we have\n[MATH: \\dfrac{\\sqrt{12}}{\\sqrt{3}} = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{12}{3}}= \\sqrt{4} = 2 .]\n\n---\n\nItem 6 \u2014 Question 3\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: (sqrt(18))/(sqrt(3)) =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: sqrt(3)]\nb. [MATH: sqrt(2)]\nc. [MATH: 3sqrt(2)]\nd. [MATH: sqrt(6)]\ne. [MATH: 2sqrt(3)]\n\n---\n\nItem 7 \u2014 Question 4\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: (sqrt(5))/(sqrt(2)) =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: sqrt((5)/(2))]\nb. [MATH: sqrt((25)/(2))]\nc. [MATH: sqrt((5)/(4))]\nd. [MATH: sqrt((25)/(8))]\ne. [MATH: sqrt(10)]\n\n---\n\nItem 8 \u2014 Example: The Quotient Rule With Cube Roots\n\nExample Prompt:\nExpress [MATH: \\dfrac{\\sqrt[3]{3}}{\\sqrt[3]{4}}] as a single radical.\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nApplying the quotient rule, we have\n[MATH: \\dfrac{\\sqrt[3]{3}}{\\sqrt[3]{4}} = \\sqrt[3] {\\dfrac{3}{4}} .]\n\n---\n\nItem 9 \u2014 Question 5\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: (root(81, 3))/(root(3, 3)) =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (9)/(root(3, 3))]\nb. [MATH: 3root(3, 3)]\nc. [MATH: 9root(3, 3)]\nd. [MATH: 9]\ne. [MATH: 3]\n\n---\n\nItem 10 \u2014 Question 6\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: (root(5, 3))/(root(7, 3)) =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: root(35, 3)]\nb. [MATH: root((25)/(49), 3)]\nc. [MATH: root((5)/(7), 3)]\nd. [MATH: (5)/(root(7, 3))]\ne. [MATH: (root(5, 3))/(7)]"
